# === 2026-08-07 user-reported 404 cleanup ===
# Two URL-shape gaps the 50-rule block above doesn't cover:
#
#   (1) English /blog/<num>-<slug>  WITHOUT a trailing slash
#       → 50-rule block matches only the with-slash form, so
#         /blog/20-wedding-highlight-reel (no slash) 404s.
#       Root cause: in _redirects, no rule rewrites /blog/X to
#       /blog/X/ before the 308 *.html catch-all. The wildcard
#       308 only fires for .html URLs, not the no-extension
#       /blog/X form. Adding explicit 301s is the cheapest fix.
#       (Could be replaced with one explicit 308 rule covering
#       /blog/<num>-* → /blog/<num>-*/, but explicit 1:1 with
#       the with-slash block keeps things obvious in the diff.)
#
#   (2) Localized /<lang>/blog/<num>-<slug>/  WITH trailing slash
#       → /<lang>/* 200 below strips <lang> first, then the
#         request hits /blog/<num>-<slug>/ — which has no static
#         file (the with-slash /blog/<num>-<slug>/ is a redirect
#         target, not a real directory). Result: 404.
#       Root cause: CF Pages 200 rewrite is a one-shot internal
#       rewrite; the rewritten path does NOT re-traverse _redirects.
#       So /<lang>/blog/<num>-slug/  →  200-rewrite  →  static lookup
#       (404, file doesn't exist) — the 301 from the 50-rule block
#       never gets a chance to fire.
#       Fix: explicit /<lang>/blog/<num>-<slug>/  →  /<lang>/blog/<slug>/
#       301 BEFORE the i18n 200 block, so the 301 wins on first-match.
#
# Both fixes are 1:1 with the 50-rule block above (same source slugs).
# Safe to delete together with the 50-rule block after 2027-01-23.

# (1) English no-slash variants of the 50 rules
# (Old i18n 200 rewrite globs removed 2026-08-08: now placed at L548-556, AFTER
#  the 500 localized 301 block, so the explicit /<lang>/blog/<num>-<slug>/ 301
#  entries win first-match. The old position at the END of the file (after all
#  dynamic globs) silently failed to fire for the user-reported localized URLs
#  — see commit 102ca1e for the diagnostic.)




